    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Since this problem asks for the number of disict equilateral triangles with at least two vertices in our set of points, we very well need to think for another possibility where one of the vertices is 'other than two'; that is to say, the third vertex of the equilateral triangle is different from two.\n\nFrom the polygon and by knowing that one of the 'top' vertices is at the same level from the first and the last vertices and the second vertex is at the same level from second and the third vertices - we get that there are $6 \\cdot 2 = 12$ such equilateral triangles. (The origin is the centre point of our polygon)\n\nHowever, since the problem also asks for the number of distinct equilateral triangles (Notice in the earlier solution we had six sets like $\\{1, 5, 3\\}$ and $\\{1, 5, 4\\}$ = two - that is correct to count them as one distinct equilateral triangle) we need to subtract the two equilateral triangles counting each as twice.\n$\\boxed{10}$. For each position of vertex $A_i$, we have two choices for $A_j$ and three choices for $A_k$ so there are $8 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 = 48$ possible choices and we need to ensure that we do not count the equilateral triangles every time. After dividing by $3$ because each triple is counted three times, we find the answer is $48/3 = 16$ triangles that satisfy.\n\nBut one of the triangles that we set up is counted twice, so we divide by two because to count $\\{1,2,3 \\}$ and $\\{3,2,1 \\}$ is the same triangle.\n\nIf we used five vertices, the answer would be 52. Why do we need to divide the answer like that?\nMy apologies for that.",
