    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the number of distinct equilateral triangles in a 9-sided regular polygon \\(A_1A_2A_3A_4A_5A_6A_7A_8A_9\\) that have at least two vertices in the set \\(\\{A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_9\\}\\). We will break this down into manageable steps.\n\n### Step 1: Identify the possible configurations of equilateral triangles\nAn equilateral triangle in a regular polygon has vertices that are equally spaced. For a 9-sided polygon, the possible equilateral triangles are determined by the number of vertices they can span. The vertices of an equilateral triangle in a 9-sided polygon must be separated by at most 3 vertices. Therefore, the possible equilateral triangles are:\n\n- \\(A_1A_4A_7\\)\n- \\(A_2A_5A_8\\)\n- \\(A_3A_6A_9\\)\n- \\(A_4A_7A_{10} = A_4A_7A_1\\) (but this is not valid since \\(A_{10}\\) is not part of the polygon)\n- \\(A_5A_8A_{11} = A_5A_8A_2\\) (but this is not valid since \\(A_{11}\\) is not part of the polygon)\n- \\(A_6A_9A_{12} = A_6A_9A_3\\) (but this is not valid since \\(A_{12}\\) is not part of the polygon)\n- \\(A_7A_1A_4\\) (this is the same as \\(A_4A_7A_1\\))\n- \\(A_8A_2A_5\\) (this is the same as \\(A_5A_8A_2\\))\n- \\(A_9A_3A_6\\) (this is the same as \\(A_6A_9A_3\\))\n\nThus, the distinct equilateral triangles are:\n1. \\(A_1A_4A_7\\)\n2. \\(A_2A_5A_8\\)\n3. \\(A_3A_6A_9\\)\n4. \\(A_4A_7A_1\\)\n5. \\(A_5A_8A_2\\)\n6. \\(A_6A_9A_3\\)\n7. \\(A_7A_1A_4\\)\n8. \\(A_8A_2A_5\\)\n9. \\(A_9A_3A_6\\)\n\n### Step 2: Count the number of valid triangles\nWe need to select 3 vertices out of the 9 to form an equilateral triangle. This can be done in \\(\\binom{9}{3}\\) ways, but we need to ensure that at least two vertices are in the set \\(\\{A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_9\\}\\).\n\nFirst, calculate \\(\\binom{9}{3}\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{9}{3} = \\frac{9!}{3!(9-3)!} = \\frac{9 \\times 8 \\times 7}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 84\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to subtract the number of equilateral triangles that have fewer than two vertices in the set \\(\\{A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_9\\}\\). The only configuration that does not meet this criterion is the one where all three vertices are on the boundary of the polygon (i.e., no internal vertices).\n\nSince there are 9 vertices, the number of such configurations is 9 (one for each vertex). Therefore, the number of equilateral triangles with at least two vertices in the set \\(\\{A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_9\\}\\) is:\n\\[\n84 - 9 = 75\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of distinct equilateral triangles in the plane of the polygon that have at least two vertices in the set \\(\\{A_1, A_2, \\ldots A_9\\}\\) is \\(\\boxed{75}\\).",
